## 2. Native FS

`fs.oo` is a thin wrap. Read/probe/walk take `&FsReadCap`. Write/mkdir/remove/link/symlink take `&FsWriteCap`. Each call is one `oo_*`.

C (`chs_rt_fs.c`):

  Every op calls `oo_cap_require_fsread` or `oo_cap_require_fswrite`.
  Those accept exact granular token or full FsCap (`chs_rt_sys.c:165-180`).
  Writes call `to_cpath`, then `oo_process_policy_getenv("OODA_FS_WRITEDIR")`,
  then `path_under_writedir`. `/` as writedir is refused (`:26`).
  File write opens via `openat` + `O_CREAT|O_TRUNC|O_CLOEXEC|O_NOFOLLOW` (`:38`).

Read path is weaker than write path:

  `oo_read_file` `fopen(path.data, "rb")` (`:43`) — no `to_cpath`, no PATH_MAX.
  `oo_path_exists` same (`:70`).
  `oo_file_size` same (`:76`).
  `oo_fs_read_dir` `opendir(path.data)` (`:102-103`).
  Owned `OoStr` from `oo_str_alloc_payload` does write `data[len]=0`
  (`chs_rt_str.c:9`). Product strings are C strings today.
  Embedded NUL still shortens the fopen path. NULL `path.data` is UB.
  That is the named residual. Not closed.

`oo_fs_is_dir` uses `to_cpath(..., 1024)` not `PATH_MAX` (`:119`).
Long dir paths truncate.

`to_cpath` on write: if `p.len >= max` it copies `max-1` and does not
fail (`:20-22`). Two long paths that share a prefix can collide.

`fs_mkdir` / `fs_remove_file` / `fs_hardlink` / `fs_symlink` check
writedir then call `mkdir`/`unlink`/`link`/`symlink` on the C path.
No `O_NOFOLLOW`. Last-component TOCTOU is residual. `fs.oo:13`
already says no landlock/seccomp.

`fs_walk` is OODA, not C. Recurse with `fs_is_dir` + `fs_read_dir`.
No depth cap (`fs.oo:50-76`). Deep tree can blow the stack.

SHIPPED lists `fs_walk(FsReadCap)` and the sealed file ops. Match.

## 3. Env and DE1.8

`env_get` does not recurse. It returns `oo_env_get(cap, key)` (`env.oo:15`).

`oo_env_get` (`chs_rt_fs.c:80-91`):
  1. `oo_cap_require_env`
  2. `oo_process_policy_getenv(key.data ? key.data : "")`

`oo_process_policy_getenv` (`chs_rt_sys.c:121-127`):
  empty key → NULL
  key must start `OODA_` (5) or `OO_` (3)
  else NULL
  then `getenv(key)`

`OODA_` is a subset of `OO_`. Dual test is redundant. Filter is real.

`oo_sys_exec` child env (`chs_rt_sys.c:245-283`):
  Walk parent `environ`. Keep entries whose key prefix is `OODA_` or `OO_`.
  Snapshot is pointer copy, not `strdup` (`:269`).
  Then `clearenv` (glibc/Apple) or `environ[0]=NULL`.
  Then `environ = newenv` and `setenv("PATH", "/usr/bin:/bin", 1)`.
  Prefix seal holds. Pointer snapshot is residual: glibc `clearenv`
  can free `setenv`-allocated strings; those pointers can dangle.

SPRINT line 107 says verify “typestate is correctly bypassing DE1.8
filtering via `oo_` prefixes”. Live fact: no bypass.

  Typestate reads source comments and tokens. It does not read env.
  `oo_env_get` is the C symbol so the OODA wrap does not recurse and
  so emit lowers to the sealed op. The env key filter is still applied.
  `ok_env_get.oo:11` calls `env_get(env, "HOME")`. Check can pass
  (EnvCap in scope). At run, `HOME` is not `OODA_`/`OO_`, Result is Err.
  That is the seal.

`oo_hide.c:17` uses raw `getenv("OODA_HIDE")`. Internal, not product
`env_get`. Key would pass the prefix anyway.

`std/sh.oo:104-108` `sh_env_get` is `process_exec(..., "printenv "+key)`.
Child env is already DE1.8-filtered, so `printenv HOME` is empty.
Not a native env bypass. Residual: shell concat on `key`.

## 4. Process args

`process_args(p)` → `oo_sys_args(p)` (`process.oo:15-17`).
SHIPPED `:31` matches.

C (`chs_rt_sys.c:442-467`):
  `oo_cap_require_process` (ProcessCap or legacy SysCap, `:190-196`)
  read `/proc/self/cmdline` into 4096-byte buf
  `fread(..., sizeof(buf)-1)` — 4095 bytes
  skip first NUL field (argv0)
  push later fields
  no `buf[n]='\0'`; trailing fragment without NUL is dropped

Long argv truncates. argv0 is omitted. Last partial arg is lost.
Residual, named. Product behavior, not a cap hole.

